;; -*- lisp-data -*-
;;
;; GENERATED by tools/make-score-data.el -- edit that, not this.
;;
;; Score's task: "how strongly does this sentence commit to the event
;; happening?", on five ordered levels
;;
;;   0 ruled out  <  1 unlikely  <  2 uncertain  <  3 likely  <  4 certain
;;
;; Sixteen scenarios, fifteen sentences each, 240 examples, exactly 48 at
;; every level and exactly three per level per scenario.  So the
;; majority-class baseline is 0.200 with nothing to round, and the best
;; constant answer is the middle level at an MAE of 1.200.  Both numbers
;; are what the head has to beat to have used the sentence at all.
;;
;; SPLIT BY :scenario, never by example.  Fifteen sentences share a subject
;; and a verb phrase; splitting inside one would put near-duplicates on both
;; sides and report memorisation as generalisation.
;;
;; Two difficulties, marked by :hard.
;;
;;   :hard nil -- one modal word carries the level ("unlikely", "certainly").
;;      Lexically separable, and the unigram baseline is reported on it so
;;      the encoder is not credited with what a lookup table does.
;;
;;   :hard t -- every sentence has a partner in the same scenario with an
;;      IDENTICAL SET OF WORDS and, in four pairs out of five, a different
;;      level.  The matrix clause already carries "not", so flipping the
;;      embedded clause's polarity adds a second "not" that a set-valued
;;      bag cannot see:
;;
;;        "I do not doubt that E will arrive"       certain
;;        "I do not doubt that E will not arrive"   ruled out
;;
;;      Two inputs a model cannot distinguish must get the same answer, so
;;      ANY bag-of-words model scores at most 0.600 accuracy and at least
;;      1.200 MAE on this subset, whatever its capacity.  That is a bound,
;;      not an expectation, and the generator recomputes it from the
;;      sentences rather than restating it from here.
;;
;; Limitation, stated rather than left to be discovered: these sentences are
;; template-regular.  A result here bounds what a frozen donor CAN carry on
;; an ordinal scale; it says nothing about messy input, and the levels are
;; assigned by template rather than annotated, so they test whether the
;; encoder recovers a rule rather than whether it agrees with a human.

(:question "How strongly does this sentence commit to the event happening?"
 :template "%s Likelihood:"
 :levels ["ruled out" "unlikely" "uncertain" "likely" "certain"]
 :examples
